data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Your code must switch from preemptive termination to **cooperative cancellation**.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running and must actually stop (recommended):

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Executes work with a timeout. The work must check the token to cooperate.
    /// </summary>
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | The first overload abandons the task but it keeps running in the background. Use the `Func<CancellationToken, T>` overload and check the token inside `work`. |
| **`CancellationTokenSource` leak** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaking it leaks internal timers. |
| **Blocking calls ignore the token** | `Thread.Sleep`, synchronous I/O won't respond to cancellation. Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (as shown). Let it propagate everywhere else. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you truly cannot modify the work to check a token, move it to a **child process** and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |

---

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, decide to continue |
| Manual `new Thread(...)` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |

The fundamental shift: **the work must voluntarily exit** — the runtime will not forcibly inject an exception anymore.
